1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,000
One sky. India saw — the Moon’s 27
wives.

2
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:10,000
Arcturus and Spica. In India, they are
Svātī and Citrā — two wives of the same
husband.

3
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:14,000
The Moon circles the sky in just over 27
days.

4
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:22,000
India split its path into 27 mansions.
Each is a wife’s house; the Moon spends
one night in each.

5
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:30,000
Dakṣa had many daughters. Twenty-seven
married the Moon god, Soma. But Soma
loved only one.

6
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:40,000
He stayed with Rohiṇī every night. The
other 26 went home to complain to their
father.

7
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:47,000
Soma promised to treat them all alike,
and didn’t. Furious, Dakṣa cursed him
with a wasting disease.

8
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:52,000
The Moon grew thinner every day. On
Earth, the herbs withered too.

9
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:59,000
The gods pleaded. Dakṣa gave in: treat
every wife equally, and bathe at
Prabhāsa, by the sea.

10
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:11,000
He lived, but the curse stayed: half the
month he wastes away, half he recovers.
These are the Moon’s phases.

11
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:15,000
Indian months are named for the full
Moon’s house.

12
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:23,000
This week’s full Moon was in Citrā, so
this month is Caitra. After dark, look
southeast for Spica.

13
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:26,000
Sardinia: stars of farmers and herders.

14
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:29,000
Follow for a new sky every week.

